  <h1 class="page-title">Structural Estimation — 10 Key Facts</h1>
  <div class="page-subtitle"><h4>Foundations, methodology and applications of structural econometric modelling</h4></div>

  <main class="content" role="main" aria-labelledby="facts-heading">
    <h2 id="facts-heading" class="section-title">Key Facts</h2>
    <ol class="fact-list" aria-label="10 key facts about structural estimation">

      <li class="fact-item">
        <span class="fact-number">1</span>
        <div class="fact-body">
          <h3>Definition: Recovering Deep Parameters from Data</h3>
          <p>Structural estimation is an econometric methodology that specifies an explicit economic model — grounded in theory — and uses observed data to recover the model's deep (or "primitive") parameters, such as preference parameters, technology coefficients, or strategic payoffs. Unlike reduced-form approaches, structural estimation imposes the restrictions of economic theory on the estimation procedure itself.</p>
          <div class="ref">Ref: Reiss &amp; Wolak (2007), "Structural Econometric Modeling," <em>Handbook of Econometrics</em>, Vol. 6A.</div>
        </div>
      </li>

      <li class="fact-item">
        <span class="fact-number">2</span>
        <div class="fact-body">
          <h3>Origins in the Cowles Commission (1940s–1950s)</h3>
          <p>The intellectual foundations of structural estimation trace back to the Cowles Commission at the University of Chicago (later Yale). Pioneering work by Haavelmo (1944), Koopmans, and Marschak established the simultaneous equations framework and the crucial distinction between structural parameters and reduced-form coefficients. Haavelmo's 1944 monograph — which earned him the 1989 Nobel Prize — formalised the probability approach to econometrics that underpins all structural work.</p>
          <div class="ref">Ref: Haavelmo (1944), "The Probability Approach in Econometrics," <em>Econometrica</em>, Supplement.</div>
        </div>
      </li>

      <li class="fact-item">
        <span class="fact-number">3</span>
        <div class="fact-body">
          <h3>The Identification Problem Is Central</h3>
          <p>A structural model is identified if and only if its deep parameters can be uniquely recovered from the distribution of observable data. Identification failures — where multiple parameter vectors are observationally equivalent — are the core challenge of structural estimation. Exclusion restrictions, functional form assumptions, and equilibrium conditions are the primary tools used to achieve identification. Without credible identification, structural estimates are not interpretable.</p>
          <div class="ref">Ref: Manski (1995), <em>Identification Problems in the Social Sciences</em>, Harvard University Press.</div>
        </div>
      </li>

      <li class="fact-item">
        <span class="fact-number">4</span>
        <div class="fact-body">
          <h3>Maximum Likelihood and GMM Are the Workhorse Estimators</h3>
          <p>The two dominant estimation strategies in structural econometrics are Maximum Likelihood Estimation (MLE) and the Generalised Method of Moments (GMM). MLE requires a fully specified likelihood function derived from the structural model and is asymptotically efficient when correctly specified. GMM — introduced by Hansen (1982) — requires only moment conditions implied by the model and is more robust to distributional misspecification, making it the preferred choice when the full likelihood is intractable.</p>
          <div class="ref">Ref: Hansen (1982), "Large Sample Properties of Generalized Method of Moments Estimators," <em>Econometrica</em>, 50(4).</div>
        </div>
      </li>

      <li class="fact-item">
        <span class="fact-number">5</span>
        <div class="fact-body">
          <h3>Simulation-Based Methods Expanded the Frontier</h3>
          <p>Many structural models generate likelihoods or moment conditions that cannot be evaluated analytically. Simulation-based methods — including Simulated Method of Moments (SMM), Indirect Inference, and Simulated Maximum Likelihood — overcome this by using Monte Carlo simulation to approximate the model's predictions. These methods, developed by McFadden (1989), Pakes &amp; Pollard (1989), and Gouriéroux, Monfort &amp; Renault (1993), dramatically expanded the class of models amenable to structural estimation.</p>
          <div class="ref">Ref: Gouriéroux, Monfort &amp; Renault (1993), "Indirect Inference," <em>Journal of Applied Econometrics</em>, 8.</div>
        </div>
      </li>

      <li class="fact-item">
        <span class="fact-number">6</span>
        <div class="fact-body">
          <h3>Counterfactual Policy Analysis Is the Key Advantage</h3>
          <p>The defining advantage of structural estimation over reduced-form methods is the ability to conduct counterfactual policy analysis. Because structural parameters are (in principle) invariant to policy changes — a property formalised by the Lucas Critique (1976) — estimated structural models can simulate the effects of policies never observed in the data. This makes structural estimation indispensable for evaluating proposed regulations, tax reforms, market redesigns, and welfare programmes before implementation.</p>
          <div class="ref">Ref: Lucas (1976), "Econometric Policy Evaluation: A Critique," <em>Carnegie-Rochester Conference Series</em>, 1.</div>
        </div>
      </li>

      <li class="fact-item">
        <span class="fact-number">7</span>
        <div class="fact-body">
          <h3>Industrial Organisation Became the Leading Application</h3>
          <p>Since the 1990s, empirical industrial organisation (IO) has been the single largest field applying structural estimation. The Berry, Levinsohn &amp; Pakes (1995) random-coefficients demand model (BLP) for differentiated products markets became one of the most influential econometric frameworks in economics. Structural IO models are now routinely used by competition authorities (DOJ, FTC, European Commission) to evaluate the price effects of proposed mergers and the welfare consequences of market power.</p>
          <div class="ref">Ref: Berry, Levinsohn &amp; Pakes (1995), "Automobile Prices in Market Equilibrium," <em>Econometrica</em>, 63(4).</div>
        </div>
      </li>

      <li class="fact-item">
        <span class="fact-number">8</span>
        <div class="fact-body">
          <h3>Dynamic Discrete Choice Models Revolutionised Labour and Development Economics</h3>
          <p>Structural estimation of dynamic discrete choice (DDC) models — where agents make sequential decisions under uncertainty — transformed labour economics, development economics, and health economics. Rust (1987) introduced the nested fixed-point algorithm to estimate the deep parameters of Harold Zurcher's bus engine replacement problem. Keane &amp; Wolpin (1997) estimated a life-cycle model of schooling, work, and occupational choice that remains a benchmark for human capital research. These models allow researchers to decompose observed behaviour into preferences, beliefs, and constraints.</p>
          <div class="ref">Ref: Rust (1987), "Optimal Replacement of GMC Bus Engines," <em>Econometrica</em>, 55(5).</div>
        </div>
      </li>

      <li class="fact-item">
        <span class="fact-number">9</span>
        <div class="fact-body">
          <h3>The Structural vs. Reduced-Form Debate Shaped Modern Econometrics</h3>
          <p>The tension between structural and reduced-form (or "design-based") approaches has been one of the most consequential methodological debates in economics. Angrist &amp; Pischke's "credibility revolution" emphasised transparent identification via natural experiments and instrumental variables, often without imposing a full structural model. Structural economists counter that reduced-form estimates, while internally valid, cannot answer out-of-sample policy questions. The contemporary consensus increasingly favours combining both: using quasi-experimental variation to identify key structural parameters.</p>
          <div class="ref">Ref: Nevo &amp; Whinston (2010), "Taking the Dogma out of Econometrics," <em>Journal of Economic Perspectives</em>, 24(2).</div>
        </div>
      </li>

      <li class="fact-item">
        <span class="fact-number">10</span>
        <div class="fact-body">
          <h3>Machine Learning Is Transforming Structural Estimation</h3>
          <p>Recent advances integrate machine learning techniques into the structural estimation pipeline. Neural networks are used as flexible function approximators to solve high-dimensional dynamic programming problems (e.g., deep reinforcement learning for value function approximation). Bayesian optimisation and automatic differentiation accelerate the estimation of computationally expensive structural models. Athey &amp; Imbens (2019) and related work demonstrate how machine learning can improve first-stage estimation (e.g., demand prediction) while preserving the economic interpretability of structural parameters in the second stage.</p>
          <div class="ref">Ref: Athey &amp; Imbens (2019), "Machine Learning Methods That Economists Should Know About," <em>Annual Review of Economics</em>, 11.</div>
        </div>
      </li>

    </ol>

    <hr class="soft-separator" aria-hidden="true">

